{"name":"Heaven","hex":"#b4ced5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Heaven","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The abode of God or the gods, when considered as a specific location; the abode of the blessed departed who reside in the presence of God or the gods","Providence, the will of God or the gods, when considered as a personal entity or specific aspect of the divine; Fate"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Heaven"}],"entries":[{"title":"Heaven","blocks":[{"text":"Heaven, or the Heavens, is a common religious cosmological or supernatural place where beings such as deities, angels, souls, saints, or venerated ancestors are said to originate, be enthroned, or reside. According to the beliefs of some religions, heavenly beings can descend to Earth or incarnate and earthly beings can ascend to Heaven in the afterlife or, in exceptional cases, enter Heaven without dying."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Paradiso_Canto_31_%28148200393%29.jpg/330px-Paradiso_Canto_31_%28148200393%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heaven","wikidata":"Q4489450"},{"title":"Heaven (Bryan Adams song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Heaven\" is a song by the Canadian singer and songwriter Bryan Adams recorded in 1983, written by Adams and Jim Vallance. It first appeared on the A Night in Heaven soundtrack album the same year and was later included on Adams' album Reckless in 1984. It was released as the third single from Reckless and reached number one on the U.S. Billboard Hot 100 in June 1985, over a year and a half after the song first appeared on record. The single was certified Gold in Canada in 1985."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5d/Bryanadams_-_Heaven_Cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heaven_(Bryan_Adams_song)","wikidata":"Q1319686"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Beowulf_-_heofones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Beowulf_-_heofones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"\"heofones\", an ancient Anglo-Saxon word for heavens in Beowulf","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeowulf_-_heofones.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg/500px-Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg/1280px-Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg/500px-Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ruins of the Ekur temple in Nippur, believed by the ancient Mesopotamians to be the \"Dur-an-ki\", the \"mooring rope\" of heaven and earth[6][7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARuins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg/500px-Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg/1280px-Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Francesco_Botticini_-_The_Assumption_of_the_Virgin.jpg/500